From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Murray <ryan@aurorasilicon.org>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 17:10:51 +1100
Subject: [PATCH] iio: aop: Add the accelerometer and gyroscope driver

Add one driver for the AOP accelerometer and gyroscope, matched by the
generic apple,aop-accel and apple,aop-gyro compatibles, so every machine
with the services shares it.

At probe the driver sends the three calibration packets the bootloader
copied from the Apple device tree (properties 75, 76 and 77, each the
location 0 entry of its container), then a 20000 us report interval. A
sample report is 22 bytes with X, Y and Z as signed little-endian 32-bit
values at byte 6, in 1/65536 g or 1/65536 degree per second. Without the
calibration the driver leaves the sensor off and does not bind.

Each sensor is an IIO device with raw X, Y and Z channels, a fixed scale
in m/s^2 or rad/s and its sampling frequency. Samples are stored under a
lock so a read never mixes two reports. A kfifo buffer takes X, Y and Z
together with a timestamp taken when the report arrives, which is what
iio-sensor-proxy and other buffer users read.

Around system sleep the driver sets the interval to zero, so the AOP does
not wake the application processor 50 times a second, and resends the
calibration and interval on wake.

Host tests run the production decoder and calibration selector against
synthetic reports and containers.

Based on Eryk Wieliczko's J713 driver (dirtyroom-linux 63e6467a), which
first got both sensors reporting at 50 Hz on the M4 MacBook Air.

